[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I reproduced connectivity from the IT HOD’s PC (it_hod, 192.168.1.2) to multiple CS department hosts (192.168.2.2–.6) and all pings succeeded with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT. it_hod’s default gateway (192.168.1.1) is present and reachable sub‑millisecond, and its local interface is up with 192.168.1.2/24 configured correctly. The successful probes show inter‑subnet routing between 192.168.1.0/24 (IT) and 192.168.2.0/24 (CS) is functioning now; TTL=63 on the replies indicates one routed hop as expected.

Because end‑to‑end ICMP reachability is currently healthy and there is no packet loss, there is no persistent fault in the switching or routing path between the IT and CS networks at this time. The earlier “timeouts” could have been transient (e.g., brief ARP/neighbor cache stale entry, momentary L3 adjacency flap, or a short‑lived host firewall state), but I could not reproduce or confirm a specific cause.

Root cause: No ongoing network fault was found; the previously reported reachability issue could not be reproduced and was likely transient.

Corrective fix: No changes are required now. If the symptom recurs, please capture the exact destination IPs/ports and time, and we can gather ARP tables and logs at that moment. As a preventive step, consider monitoring gateway adjacency and clearing ARP/neighbor cache on it_hod if intermittent timeouts reappear.
=== END ===